<article class="article">
<header class="title-block"><p class="eyebrow">Research & Learning Insights · Evidence review</p><h1>AI in Second-Language Learning: <em>What Can It Actually Help With?</em></h1><p class="dek">A systematic evidence synthesis separating the functions with credible learning support from the functions that remain overclaimed, under-tested or risky.</p><div class="meta-line"><span>63 scholarly sources reviewed</span><span>Meta-analyses · systematic reviews · controlled studies</span><span>Updated August 2026</span></div></header>

<aside class="key-points"><h2>Answer in one paragraph</h2><dl><dt>Strongest</dt><dd>AI is best supported as a <strong>practice-and-feedback layer</strong>: automated writing feedback, pronunciation/ASR, structured conversational practice, vocabulary support and formative assessment all have positive quantitative evidence.<a class="cite" href="#ref-3">3</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-6">6</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-7">7</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-8">8</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-11">11</a></dd><dt>Promising</dt><dd>GenAI can increase speaking turns, reduce the social cost of practice, scaffold revision and support self-regulation, but effects are highly dependent on task design and learner behaviour.<a class="cite" href="#ref-4">4</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-21">21</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-22">22</a></dd><dt>Weakest</dt><dd>There is much less direct evidence that present-day GenAI reliably improves <strong>listening, reading, long-term retention, transfer to spontaneous human interaction, or learning in languages other than English</strong>.<a class="cite" href="#ref-13">13</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-14">14</a></dd><dt>Core rule</dt><dd>The learner should still have to <strong>notice, retrieve, decide, speak, write and revise</strong>. If AI does the language production instead, immediate task performance can improve while the amount of learning work performed by the learner shrinks.<a class="cite" href="#ref-54">54</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-55">55</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-56">56</a></dd></dl></aside>

<section class="section" id="summary"><p class="section-kicker">01 · Executive summary</p><h2>AI is not one teaching method</h2><p class="lead">The phrase “AI for language learning” combines technologies that do very different things. Automatic speech recognition listens. Automated writing evaluation detects and classifies text features. Machine translation transforms one language into another. Chatbots generate dialogue. Large language models generate explanations, examples, feedback and text. Their evidence therefore has to be judged function by function.</p><p>The broadest recent GenAI meta-analysis synthesized <strong>51 studies and 175 independent effect sizes</strong> and reported large pooled effects for language achievement and affective-cognitive outcomes.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">1</a> That is encouraging, but not a license to conclude that any AI feature causes large gains. The same literature reports large heterogeneity, highly variable controls, a fast-moving technology base and a research corpus concentrated in higher education and English.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">1</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-13">13</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-14">14</a></p><div class="finding-grid"><div class="finding"><span class="number">0.69</span><p>Meta-analytic effect of ASR on ESL/EFL pronunciation.<a class="cite" href="#ref-7">7</a></p></div><div class="finding"><span class="number">0.61</span><p>Overall chatbot effect on second/foreign-language learning.<a class="cite" href="#ref-3">3</a></p></div><div class="finding"><span class="number">0.39</span><p>AI-enabled assessment effect on language learning.<a class="cite" href="#ref-11">11</a></p></div></div><div class="callout"><span class="label">The useful framing</span><p>Ask <strong>“What learning action is the AI increasing or improving?”</strong> rather than “Does AI work?” The best-supported answers are: more feedback, more speaking turns, more pronunciation attempts, more revision cycles, targeted vocabulary practice and faster formative information.</p></div></section>

<section class="section" id="method"><p class="section-kicker">02 · Review method</p><h2>Evidence was weighted by what the study actually measured</h2><p>This review prioritizes meta-analyses and systematic reviews, then controlled and quasi-experimental L2 studies, and finally established SLA theory needed to explain mechanisms. Searches covered generative AI, ChatGPT/LLMs, conversational agents, ASR, automated writing evaluation, AI-enabled assessment and machine translation. General educational AI studies were not treated as language-learning evidence unless they directly informed a mechanism.</p><div class="limitations"><h3>Interpretive safeguards</h3><ul><li><strong>Performance is not automatically learning.</strong> A better essay produced with AI may reflect assistance during the task rather than a durable increase in unaided writing ability.</li><li><strong>Tools change faster than trials.</strong> Findings from GPT-3.5, older ASR engines or rule-based AWE cannot be transferred perfectly to current multimodal models.</li><li><strong>English dominates.</strong> Evidence for French, Mandarin, Spanish and less commonly taught languages is far thinner.</li><li><strong>Very large effects deserve scrutiny.</strong> Early-stage literatures often contain small samples, weak controls and strong novelty effects.<a class="cite" href="#ref-59">59</a></li></ul></div></section>

<section class="section" id="map"><p class="section-kicker">03 · Capability map</p><h2>What can AI actually help with?</h2><div class="rating-grid"><div class="rating-card"><span class="rating">Strong evidence</span><h3>Writing feedback & revision</h3><p>AWE meta-analyses consistently show positive writing effects, particularly for accuracy and surface-level revision. Human or teacher-mediated feedback remains important for rhetoric, argument and prioritization.<a class="cite" href="#ref-8">8</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-9">9</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-47">47</a></p></div><div class="rating-card"><span class="rating">Strong evidence</span><h3>Pronunciation & speech practice</h3><p>ASR improves pronunciation overall, especially segmental targets when feedback is explicit and practice is sustained.<a class="cite" href="#ref-7">7</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-19">19</a></p></div><div class="rating-card"><span class="rating">Moderate evidence</span><h3>Conversational speaking</h3><p>Chatbots increase low-stakes interaction; controlled studies show gains in oral proficiency and reductions in anxiety, but transfer to human conversation is less established.<a class="cite" href="#ref-3">3</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-22">22</a></p></div><div class="rating-card"><span class="rating">Promising / heterogeneous</span><h3>Vocabulary</h3><p>AI vocabulary interventions show large pooled gains, but heterogeneity is extreme and enriched non-AI multimedia controls reduce the apparent advantage.<a class="cite" href="#ref-6">6</a></p></div><div class="rating-card"><span class="rating">Moderate evidence</span><h3>Formative assessment</h3><p>AI assessment can produce useful, rapid diagnostic information and improve learning when it is iterative and instructionally connected.<a class="cite" href="#ref-11">11</a></p></div><div class="rating-card"><span class="rating">Under-researched</span><h3>Reading & listening</h3><p>These skills appear frequently in AI claims but much less often in rigorous comparative research; systematic reviews explicitly identify them as gaps.<a class="cite" href="#ref-14">14</a></p></div></div>
<figure class="figure"><p class="figure-number">Figure 1 · Author synthesis</p><h3 class="figure-title">The most defensible AI learning loop</h3><div class="pathway"><div class="pathway-step"><strong>Learner attempts</strong><span>Speak, write, retrieve, choose or interpret before seeing the answer.</span></div><div class="pathway-step"><strong>AI responds</strong><span>Feedback, transcription, hint, comparison, follow-up question or targeted example.</span></div><div class="pathway-step"><strong>Learner revises</strong><span>Explain the error, repair output, try again and make a new decision.</span></div><div class="pathway-step"><strong>Delayed transfer</strong><span>Use the form again without the AI in a new task, person or context.</span></div></div><figcaption>Author synthesis based on corrective-feedback, interaction, output, noticing and sociocultural accounts.<a class="cite" href="#ref-53">53</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-54">54</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-55">55</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-56">56</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-57">57</a></figcaption></figure></section>

<section class="section" id="writing"><p class="section-kicker">04 · Writing & feedback</p><h2>The clearest case is not “AI writes better”—it is “AI can create more revision cycles”</h2><p class="lead">Writing is the most heavily researched area of GenAI language learning, but it is also where performance and learning are easiest to confuse.</p><p>Pre-GenAI automated writing evaluation already had a substantial evidence base. Zhai and Ma found a large overall effect on writing quality, and later syntheses similarly report benefits for EFL/ESL writing.<a class="cite" href="#ref-8">8</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-9">9</a> New LLMs broaden feedback from grammar and mechanics toward explanations and rhetorical comments, but higher-order feedback is still less dependable than surface correction.<a class="cite" href="#ref-47">47</a></p><p>Teacher-in-the-loop designs are particularly defensible. Han and Li used ChatGPT to help teachers generate detailed corrective and rhetorical feedback for large EFL classes rather than delivering unreviewed AI comments directly to students.<a class="cite" href="#ref-29">29</a> This uses AI to increase feedback capacity while retaining human judgment.</p><div class="warning callout"><span class="label">Important distinction</span><p><strong>Assisted text quality ≠ unaided writing development.</strong> Woo et al. found that AI-generated text could contribute to higher-quality student products, but learner competence strongly shaped how well students used it.<a class="cite" href="#ref-30">30</a> The educational question is whether students can later plan, formulate and revise without the model.</p></div><div class="mechanisms"><div class="mechanism"><span class="index">01</span><h3>Error detection</h3><p>AI can flag grammar, spelling, lexical and some discourse problems immediately, increasing the number of feedback events per learner.<a class="cite" href="#ref-8">8</a></p></div><div class="mechanism"><span class="index">02</span><h3>Explanation on demand</h3><p>LLMs can rephrase a correction, generate contrasts and answer follow-up questions; quality depends on the model and prompt, as shown in L2 Chinese feedback research.<a class="cite" href="#ref-31">31</a></p></div><div class="mechanism"><span class="index">03</span><h3>Revision support</h3><p>The useful outcome is a better learner revision, not merely a better model rewrite. AWE research repeatedly finds that uptake and revision behaviour mediate value.<a class="cite" href="#ref-35">35</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-37">37</a></p></div></div></section>

<section class="section" id="speaking"><p class="section-kicker">05 · Speaking & pronunciation</p><h2>AI can manufacture practice opportunities that many learners otherwise do not get</h2><p>For pronunciation, the evidence is mature enough to be specific. ASR has a medium overall effect, with stronger results when systems provide <strong>explicit corrective information</strong>, target segmental pronunciation and are used for more than a very short exposure.<a class="cite" href="#ref-7">7</a> Dynamic-assessment designs can further reduce anxiety and cognitive load by scaffolding feedback instead of simply marking an attempt wrong.<a class="cite" href="#ref-23">23</a></p><p>Conversational GenAI adds another affordance: a learner can create dozens of speaking turns without waiting for a teacher or partner. In one randomized four-week trial, LLM dialogue improved oral proficiency, willingness to communicate and self-efficacy while lowering foreign-language anxiety.<a class="cite" href="#ref-22">22</a> Meta-analysis of informal GenAI learning likewise found its strongest skill-specific effect for speaking.<a class="cite" href="#ref-4">4</a></p><div class="callout"><span class="label">What it does not prove</span><p>A conversation with a patient, always-available bot is not psychologically identical to a conversation with a human interlocutor. Evidence for <strong>far transfer to spontaneous, socially consequential human interaction</strong> remains much thinner than evidence for practice performance and anxiety reduction.</p></div></section>

<section class="section" id="vocab"><p class="section-kicker">06 · Vocabulary & language knowledge</p><h2>Personalization can help vocabulary—but the huge effects should be read with caution</h2><p>The 2026 AI-vocabulary meta-analysis reported a very large pooled effect, <strong>g = 1.309</strong>, across 18 studies. Yet the same analysis reported <strong>I² = 93.868%</strong>, meaning results varied enormously across studies.<a class="cite" href="#ref-6">6</a> The relative advantage also shrank when AI was compared with multimedia-supported learning rather than traditional instruction.</p><figure class="source-figure"><div class="source-figure-head"><p class="figure-number">Published research figure · CC BY</p><h3 class="figure-title">AI-supported vocabulary interventions vary dramatically across studies</h3></div><div class="source-figure-media"><div class="embed-safe-figure" role="img" aria-label="Forest plot of AI-supported vocabulary learning effect sizes"><strong>Published research figure 1</strong><span>Forest plot of AI-supported vocabulary learning effect sizes. The original published figure is identified and attributed in the caption below; the image payload is intentionally not embedded here so website security filters do not block this code.</span></div></div><figcaption>Forest plot reproduced from Şimşek et al. (2026), <em>Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence</em>, CC BY. The pooled effect is large, but the wide dispersion is the important design message: AI is not a uniform treatment.<a class="cite" href="#ref-6">6</a></figcaption></figure><p>Pedagogically, the strongest vocabulary use is likely to combine AI’s generative flexibility with established learning mechanisms: tailored examples, retrieval questions, contrastive examples, spaced re-encounters and required learner production. Open-ended chat without retrieval or delayed testing may feel rich while producing less durable memory.</p></section>

<section class="section" id="assessment"><p class="section-kicker">07 · Assessment & personalization</p><h2>Fast formative information is useful; high-stakes judgment is a different problem</h2><p>AI-enabled language assessment has a moderate positive learning effect in meta-analysis, but the educational gains come primarily from using assessment <strong>formatively</strong>—to trigger another learning action.<a class="cite" href="#ref-11">11</a> A score is useful when it tells the learner what to try next; a score that is opaque or inaccurate can simply automate confusion.</p><p>“Personalization” should also be defined precisely. AI can change difficulty, examples, pacing, feedback language and practice topics quickly. What remains much less established is whether a generic LLM accurately models an individual learner’s evolving interlanguage over months. Persistent learner models, validated diagnostics and controlled progression are stronger forms of personalization than remembering a few conversational preferences.</p></section>

<section class="section" id="reading"><p class="section-kicker">08 · Reading & listening</p><h2>Useful support tools, but surprisingly little direct causal evidence</h2><p>AI can simplify passages, define words, generate comprehension questions, produce transcripts, create listening scripts and answer questions about a text. These are plausible supports. Yet the main systematic reviews of post-ChatGPT language education repeatedly report that <strong>reading and listening are under-studied compared with writing</strong>.<a class="cite" href="#ref-13">13</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-14">14</a></p><p>That gap matters. A chatbot that explains a reading passage may improve task completion while reducing the amount of inferencing the learner performs. A transcript may help listening comprehension while decreasing attention to the acoustic signal. Good design therefore uses assistance contingently—after an attempt, at the point of breakdown, and with later removal of support.</p></section>

<section class="section" id="mechanisms"><p class="section-kicker">09 · Why AI can help</p><h2>The useful mechanisms are older than ChatGPT</h2><div class="mechanisms"><div class="mechanism"><span class="index">01</span><h3>More interaction</h3><p>Interactionist theory predicts benefits when learners negotiate meaning and modify output. AI can dramatically increase access to such exchanges if tasks require genuine learner responses.<a class="cite" href="#ref-54">54</a></p></div><div class="mechanism"><span class="index">02</span><h3>More output</h3><p>Producing language forces learners to test what they can say. AI helps when it elicits output; it can hurt when it supplies the output before the learner tries.<a class="cite" href="#ref-55">55</a></p></div><div class="mechanism"><span class="index">03</span><h3>More noticing</h3><p>Highlighting contrasts, errors and alternatives can focus attention on form. But automatic rewriting can hide the exact gap the learner needed to notice.<a class="cite" href="#ref-56">56</a></p></div><div class="mechanism"><span class="index">04</span><h3>More individualized feedback</h3><p>Feedback is powerful when it is timely, specific and actionable. AI lowers the marginal cost of delivering another feedback cycle, although correctness still has to be monitored.<a class="cite" href="#ref-51">51</a></p></div></div>
<figure class="source-figure"><div class="source-figure-head"><p class="figure-number">Published research figure · CC BY 4.0</p><h3 class="figure-title">The early GenAI evidence is positive—and highly dispersed</h3></div><div class="source-figure-media"><div class="embed-safe-figure" role="img" aria-label="Forest plot of GenAI language achievement effect sizes"><strong>Published research figure 2</strong><span>Forest plot of GenAI language achievement effect sizes. The original published figure is identified and attributed in the caption below; the image payload is intentionally not embedded here so website security filters do not block this code.</span></div></div><figcaption>Language-achievement forest plot reproduced from Saarela, Gunasekara & Kumarage (2026), <em>Discover Computing</em>, CC BY 4.0. The plot visually demonstrates why pooled headline effects should not be treated as a single expected result for every learner or product.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">1</a></figcaption></figure></section>

<section class="section" id="risks"><p class="section-kicker">10 · Limits, failure modes & safeguards</p><h2>The same flexibility that makes GenAI useful also creates its main educational risks</h2><div class="limitations"><h3>Six recurring problems in the literature</h3><ul><li><strong>Incorrect or invented language information.</strong> Fluent explanations can be wrong, incomplete or overconfident; systematic reviews identify content quality as a core challenge.<a class="cite" href="#ref-13">13</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-14">14</a></li><li><strong>Over-reliance.</strong> If the learner asks AI to produce the answer, they may complete the task with less retrieval, formulation and monitoring of their own language.</li><li><strong>Model drift.</strong> Feedback quality can change with model version; L2 Chinese research found meaningful version effects.<a class="cite" href="#ref-31">31</a></li><li><strong>Uneven benefit.</strong> More proficient learners may be better positioned to judge, prompt and integrate AI output, potentially widening rather than closing gaps.<a class="cite" href="#ref-30">30</a></li><li><strong>Privacy and governance.</strong> Language practice can contain personal, educational or assessment data; review literature repeatedly flags privacy leakage and unclear data handling.<a class="cite" href="#ref-14">14</a></li><li><strong>Construct substitution.</strong> AI can make a product look better without improving the underlying skill—for example, rewriting an essay rather than teaching the learner to write one.</li></ul></div><div class="warning callout"><span class="label">A practical test</span><p>After using the AI, remove it. <strong>Can the learner now perform better on a new item, in a delayed session, with a different prompt or with a human partner?</strong> If not, the system may have optimized task completion rather than learning.</p></div></section>

<section class="section" id="design"><p class="section-kicker">11 · Design implications</p><h2>Use AI to increase productive struggle, not erase it</h2><div class="implications"><h3>Evidence-aligned design rules</h3><ol><li><strong>Attempt before assistance.</strong> Require the learner to answer, speak, translate, predict or draft before the model reveals a solution.</li><li><strong>Prefer feedback over replacement.</strong> “Here is what to fix and why” is usually more educational than “Here is a better paragraph.”</li><li><strong>Make feedback actionable.</strong> Ask for a revised attempt immediately and revisit the same issue later.</li><li><strong>Use constrained roles.</strong> A pronunciation coach, examiner, Socratic tutor or error explainer is easier to evaluate than a generic “AI teacher.”</li><li><strong>Keep a human escalation path.</strong> Teachers should handle ambiguity, higher-order discourse, persistent misconceptions and high-stakes decisions.</li><li><strong>Measure delayed unaided transfer.</strong> Platform scores during AI assistance are insufficient evidence of learning.</li><li><strong>Log model/version and prompts.</strong> Replicability requires knowing which system produced the feedback.</li><li><strong>Do not generalize English findings automatically.</strong> French and other target languages require direct validation of feedback, speech recognition and cultural/pragmatic quality.</li></ol></div></section>

<section class="section" id="matrix"><p class="section-kicker">12 · Evidence matrix</p><h2>All 63 sources, with the conclusion used in this review</h2><p>The table makes the review auditable. Each “main conclusion” is a concise paraphrase of the finding or synthesis relevant to AI-supported second-language learning; it is not a quotation from the paper.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>63-source evidence matrix</caption><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Study</th><th>Publication</th><th>Main conclusion used here</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Saarela, Gunasekara &amp; Kumarage (2026)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">GenAI meta-analysis</span></td><td>A meta-analysis of generative AI effects on language proficiency and affective–cognitive outcomes in language learning. Discover Computing, 29, 116.</td><td>Across 51 studies and 175 effect sizes, GenAI interventions produced large pooled effects for language achievement (g = 1.111) and affective–cognitive outcomes (g = 1.029), but the authors stress substantial heterogeneity and moderator dependence.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10791-026-10015-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Wang (2024)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AI meta-analysis</span></td><td>Artificial Intelligence in L2 learning: A meta-analysis of contextual, instructional, and social-emotional moderators. System, 126, 103498.</td><td>A broad synthesis of AI-supported L2 learning reported an overall moderate-to-large benefit (SMD about .98) and found that environment, duration, educational level, interactivity and feedback source help explain wide variation in outcomes.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.system.2024.103498" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Lyu, Lai &amp; Guo (2025)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Chatbot meta-analysis</span><