ching: a meta-analysis study. Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence, 9, 1806700.</td><td>Eighteen studies (23 effects; 1,823 participants) produced a large vocabulary effect (g = 1.309), but heterogeneity was extreme (I² = 93.868%), making context and comparator crucial to interpretation.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1806700" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><strong>Ngo, Chen &amp; Lai (2024)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">ASR meta-analysis</span></td><td>The effectiveness of automatic speech recognition in ESL/EFL pronunciation: A meta-analysis. ReCALL, 36(1), 4–21.</td><td>Across 15 studies and 38 effects, ASR produced a medium pronunciation benefit (g = .69). Explicit corrective feedback, segmental targets, longer treatments and peer-supported practice were associated with stronger outcomes.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1017/S0958344023000113" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><strong>Zhai &amp; Ma (2023)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AWE meta-analysis</span></td><td>The Effectiveness of Automated Writing Evaluation on Writing Quality: A Meta-Analysis. Journal of Educational Computing Research, 61(4), 875–900.</td><td>Twenty-six studies with 2,468 participants showed a large AWE effect on writing quality (g = .861), with stronger benefits for post-secondary and EFL/ESL learners.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1177/07356331221127300" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><strong>Ngo, Chen &amp; Lai (2024)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AWE meta-analysis</span></td><td>The effectiveness of automated writing evaluation in EFL/ESL writing: a three-level meta-analysis. Interactive Learning Environments, 32(2), 727–744.</td><td>A three-level synthesis found automated writing evaluation beneficial for EFL/ESL writing, while emphasizing that effects depend on learner, feedback and implementation characteristics rather than the software alone.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/10494820.2022.2096642" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><strong>Huang, Xu, Li &amp; Yu (2024)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AWE meta-analysis</span></td><td>A Meta-analysis of Effects of Automated Writing Evaluation on Anxiety, Motivation, and Second Language Writing Skills. The Asia-Pacific Education Researcher.</td><td>The synthesis concludes that AWE can improve L2 writing while also influencing affective outcomes; it recommends combining automated and human rating/feedback rather than treating AWE as a complete substitute.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s40299-024-00865-y" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td><strong>Chen et al. (2025)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Assessment meta-analysis</span></td><td>A systematic review and meta-analysis of AI-enabled assessment in language learning: Design, implementation, and effectiveness. Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 41, e13064.</td><td>Twenty-five studies yielded a medium learning effect (g = .390). Formative/iterative assessment design, duration, educational level and learner context moderated the value of AI-enabled assessment.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1111/jcal.13064" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td><strong>Lee (2023)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Machine translation review</span></td><td>The effectiveness of machine translation in foreign language education: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Computer Assisted Language Learning, 36(1–2), 103–125.</td><td>Machine translation can support foreign-language writing and vocabulary when learners critically compare, edit and reflect on output; uncritical substitution