research.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.caeo.2024.100174" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td><strong>Bao et al. (2025)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Systematic review</span></td><td>A systematic review of AI in second language acquisition using the expanded SAMR model (2015–2024). Discover Computing, 28, 292.</td><td>The review argues that AI use in SLA ranges from simple substitution to task transformation; pedagogical value increases when AI changes the quality of interaction or feedback rather than merely automating an old worksheet.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10791-025-09833-6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td><strong>Li, Shadiev &amp; Chiu (2026)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Systematic review / meta-analysis</span></td><td>Applying Generative Artificial Intelligence to Task-based Language Teaching and Learning: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis. TechTrends, 70, 150–163.</td><td>GenAI can support task preparation, interaction and feedback in task-based language learning, but effectiveness depends on task design, learner agency and how much cognitive work remains with the learner.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s11528-025-01140-7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td><strong>Huang, Hew &amp; Fryer (2022)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Systematic review</span></td><td>Chatbots for language learning—Are they really useful? A systematic review of chatbot-supported language learning. Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 38(1), 237–257.</td><td>Twenty-five empirical studies showed technological, pedagogical and social affordances for language practice, but also highlighted shallow dialogue, design constraints and the need for theory-informed chatbot implementation.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1111/jcal.12610" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td><strong>Liu, Ab Rahman &amp; Mohamad Zain (2025)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Systematic review</span></td><td>A systematic literature review of research on automatic speech recognition in EFL pronunciation. Cogent Education, 12, 2466288.</td><td>Twenty-four empirical studies largely supported ASR for pronunciation accuracy, especially segmentals, while revealing weak theoretical grounding, limited teacher perspectives and benefits from combining ASR with peer or other feedback.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/2331186X.2025.2466288" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td><strong>AI-driven chatbot review (2025)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Systematic review</span></td><td>AI-driven chatbots in second language education: A systematic review of their efficacy and pedagogical implications. Ampersand, 14, 100224.</td><td>Thirty empirical studies suggested the clearest chatbot benefits in productive skills, particularly speaking and writing, through practice volume, rapid feedback and lower-stakes interaction.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.amper.2025.100224" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td><strong>Wang, Zou, Du &amp; Wang (2024)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Speaking experiment</span></td><td>The impact of different conversational generative AI chatbots on EFL learners: willingness to communicate, speaking anxiety, self-perceived communicative competence, and performance. System, 127, 103533.</td><td>Conversational GenAI conditions improved speaking-related outcomes relative to a conventional class comparison, illustrating that voice-capable chatbots can create additional low-stakes speaking turns rather than only text practice.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.system.2024.103533" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td><strong>Zheng, Zhou, Chen &amp; Ye (2025)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Speaking RCT</span></td><td>The influence of large language models as collaborative dialogue partners on EFL English oral proficiency and foreign language anxiety. Computer Assisted Language Learning.</td><td>In a four-week randomized study with 83 learners, GPT-4 dialogue practice improved oral proficiency, willingness to communicate and self-efficacy while reducing foreign-language anxiety; technical delays and topic drift remained practical limits.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/09588221.2025.2453191" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td><strong>Chen, Koong &amp; Liao (2022)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">ASR experiment</span></td><td>Influences of Integrating Dynamic Assessment into a Speech Recognition Learning Design to Support Students’ English Speaking Skills, Learning Anxiety and Cognitive Load. Educational Technology &amp; Society, 25(1), 1–14.</td><td>Both speech-recognition conditions improved speaking and lowered anxiety, while dynamic-assessment scaffolding reduced performance anxiety and extraneous cognitive load more than simple corrective-feedback ASR.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.30191/ETS.202201_25(1).0001" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td><strong>Liakin, Cardoso &amp; Liakina (2017)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">French pronunciation</span></td><td>Learning L2 pronunciation with a mobile speech recognizer: French /y/. CALICO Journal.</td><td>Mobile ASR-based practice helped learners notice and practice a difficult French vowel; the study illustrates how speech technology can target specific segmental contrasts rather than provide a generic ‘accent score.’</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1558/cj.26909" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td><strong>McCrocklin (2016)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">ASR / autonomy</span></td><td>Pronunciation learner autonomy: The potential of automatic speech recognition. System, 57, 25–42.</td><td>ASR can increase opportunities for autonomous pronunciation practice and self-monitoring, but learners need training to interpret recognition errors and convert them into useful pronunciation decisions.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.system.2015.12.013" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td><strong>Chen (2024)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Vocabulary / semantics</span></td><td>The AI chatbot interaction for semantic learning: A collaborative note-taking approach with EFL students. Language Learning &amp; Technology, 28(1), 1–25.</td><td>Combining AI-chatbot interaction with collaborative note-taking supported semantic learning and learner-related outcomes, suggesting that the pedagogical wrapper around the chatbot matters as much as conversational access itself.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.64152/10125/73586" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td><strong>Chen (2025)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Vocabulary / semantics</span></td><td>Improving English semantic learning outcomes through AI chatbot-based ARCS approach. Interactive Learning Environments, 33(6), 3909–3924.</td><td>An ARCS-motivational design around an AI chatbot improved semantic learning by structuring attention, relevance, confidence and satisfaction rather than relying on open-ended chatting alone.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/10494820.2023.2279228" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td><strong>Fryer &amp; Carpenter (2006)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Foundational chatbot</span></td><td>Bots as language learning tools. Language Learning &amp; Technology, 10(3), 8–14.</td><td>Early chatbot work showed why bots are attractive for low-pressure language interaction, while also exposing the conversational shallowness and breakdowns of pre-LLM systems.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://www.lltjournal.org/item/10125-44068/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td><strong>Han &amp; Li (2024)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Writing / feedback</span></td><td>Exploring ChatGPT-supported teacher feedback in the EFL context. System, 126, 103502.</td><td>A teacher-in-the-loop design used ChatGPT to help instructors produce corrective and rhetorical feedback at scale, illustrating a stronger use case than giving unfiltered model feedback directly to learners.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.system.2024.103502" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td><strong>Woo et al. (2024)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Writing / GenAI</span></td><td>Exploring AI-generated text in student writing: How does AI help? Language Learning &amp; Technology, 28(2), 183–209.</td><td>Among 23 secondary EFL writers, both human- and AI-generated words contributed to writing scores, but learner competence shaped how well AI text was integrated; AI did not erase proficiency differences.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.64152/10125/73577" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td><strong>Yang &amp; Chen (2026)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Writing / corrective feedback</span></td><td>ChatGPT and L2 Chinese writing: evaluating the impact of model version and prompt language on automated corrective feedback. Computer Assisted Language Learning, 39(4), 1119–1147.</td><td>ChatGPT-4 generated more accurate, detailed and understandable corrective feedback than 3.5 on L2 Chinese errors; model version mattered more than whether prompts were written in Chinese or English.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/09588221.2025.2453205" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td><strong>Shi, Chai, Zhou &amp; Aubrey (2025)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Writing comparison</span></td><td>Comparing the effects of ChatGPT and automated writing evaluation on students’ writing and ideal L2 writing self. Computer Assisted Language Learning.</td><td>Comparative work suggests generative feedback and conventional AWE do not function identically: the nature of feedback, learner uptake and motivational effects should be assessed separately rather than treating all AI writing tools as one category.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/09588221.2025.2458331" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td><strong>Barrot (2021)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AWE experiment</span></td><td>Using automated written corrective feedback in the writing classrooms: Effects on L2 writing accuracy. Computer Assisted Language Learning.</td><td>Automated corrective feedback can improve L2 writing accuracy when learners revise from it, but benefits depend on feedback accuracy, learner interpretation and repeated revision opportunities.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/09588221.2021.1936071" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td><strong>Dikli &amp; Bleyle (2014)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AWE / human comparison</span></td><td>Automated essay scoring feedback for second language writers: How does it compare to instructor feedback? Assessing Writing, 22, 1–17.</td><td>Automated and instructor feedback differ in focus and usefulness; machine feedback can handle recurring surface issues efficiently but does not fully reproduce human rhetorical judgment.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asw.2014.03.006" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td><strong>Stevenson &amp; Phakiti (2014)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AWE experiment</span></td><td>The effects of computer-generated feedback on the quality of writing. Assessing Writing, 19, 51–65.</td><td>Computer-generated feedback supported revision, but writing improvement depended on whether learners engaged with and understood the feedback rather than on feedback availability alone.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asw.2013.11.007" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>36</td><td><strong>Wang, Shang &amp; Briody (2013)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AWE experiment</span></td><td>Exploring the impact of using automated writing evaluation in English as a foreign language university students’ writing. Computer Assisted Language Learning, 26(3), 234–257.</td><td>AWE increased revision opportunities and could support EFL university writing, while learners still valued human guidance for issues that automated systems handled poorly.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/09588221.2012.655300" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td><strong>Li, Link &amp; Hegelheimer (2015)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AWE pedagogy</span></td><td>Rethinking the role of automated writing evaluation (AWE) feedback in ESL writing instruction. Journal of Second Language Writing, 27, 1–18.</td><td>AWE is most useful as part of a feedback ecology: students selectively use machine feedback and benefit when teachers help them interpret, prioritize and revise from it.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jslw.2014.10.004" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>38</td><td><strong>Link, Mehrzad &amp; Rahimi (2020)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AWE pedagogy<