 of Computer Assisted Learning, 37(5), 1494–1507.</td><td>Learner-centred AWE use can support writing development, but students vary in the strategies they use to interpret and incorporate automated feedback.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1111/jcal.12587" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>42</td><td><strong>Sun &amp; Fan (2022)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AWE / anxiety</span></td><td>The effects of an AWE-aided assessment approach on business English writing performance and writing anxiety. Studies in Educational Evaluation, 72, 101123.</td><td>AWE-assisted assessment improved business-English writing and influenced writing anxiety, showing that feedback technology can affect both performance and emotion.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.stueduc.2021.101123" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>43</td><td><strong>Tan, Cho &amp; Xu (2022)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AWE + peer feedback</span></td><td>Exploring the effects of automated written corrective feedback, computer-mediated peer feedback and their combination on EFL writing performance. Interactive Learning Environments.</td><td>Automated and peer feedback offer different information; combined modes can broaden revision support compared with relying on either source alone.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/10494820.2022.2034886" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>44</td><td><strong>Wilson &amp; Roscoe (2020)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AWE evaluation</span></td><td>Automated writing evaluation and feedback: Multiple metrics of efficacy. Journal of Educational Computing Research, 58(1), 87–125.</td><td>AWE effectiveness varies depending on which outcome is measured—quality, revision, motivation or engagement—so a single score is an inadequate evaluation of instructional value.</td><td><a class="source