">AWE systematic review</span></td><td>Effectiveness of automated writing evaluation systems in school settings: A systematic review of studies from 2000 to 2020. Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 38(2), 599–620.</td><td>School-based AWE can improve writing under some conditions, but effects are heterogeneous and stronger designs are needed to determine which features and instructional supports cause gains.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1111/jcal.12635" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>49</td><td><strong>Lv, Ren &amp; Xie (2021)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Feedback meta-analysis</span></td><td>The effects of online feedback on ESL/EFL writing: A meta-analysis. The Asia-Pacific Education Researcher, 30(6).</td><td>Online feedback has a positive overall effect on L2 writing, reinforcing the broader principle that immediacy and revisability matter, regardless of whether the feedback agent is fully automated.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s40299-021-00594-6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>50</td><td><strong>Mohsen (2022)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Feedback meta-analysis</span></td><td>Computer-mediated corrective feedback to improve L2 writing skills: A meta-analysis. Journal of Educational Computing Research, 60(5).</td><td>Computer-mediated corrective feedback improves L2 writing, but moderator patterns show that technology is an interaction channel, not a guarantee of useful feedback.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1177/07356331211064066" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>51</td><td><strong>Hattie &amp; Timperley (2007)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Feedback theory</span></td><td>The power of feedback. Review of Educational Research, 77(1), 81–112.</td><td>Feedback is most effective when it reduces the gap between current and desired performance 