><td>55</td><td><strong>Swain (1985)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Output hypothesis</span></td><td>Communicative competence: Some roles of comprehensible input and comprehensible output in its development. In Input in Second Language Acquisition.</td><td>Producing language can push learners to notice gaps and test hypotheses. AI is most educationally useful when it elicits learner output rather than simply generating fluent text for them.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://www.worldcat.org/title/communicative-competence-some-roles-of-comprehensible-input-and-comprehensible-output-in-its-development/oclc/12163482" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>56</td><td><strong>Schmidt (1995)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Noticing theory</span></td><td>Consciousness and foreign language learning: A tutorial on the role of attention and awareness in learning. Attention and Awareness in Foreign Language Learning.</td><td>Attention and noticing are central to converting input into learning; AI explanations and error highlighting help when they direct attention, but answer-generation can do the opposite by removing the need to notice.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://nflrc.hawaii.edu/PDFs/SCHMIDT%20A%20tutorial%20on%20the%20role%20of%20attention%20and%20awareness%20in%20learning.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>57</td><td><strong>Lantolf, Thorne &amp; Poehner (2020)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Sociocultural theory</span></td><td>Sociocultural theory and second language development. In Theories in Second Language Acquisition (3rd ed.).</td><td>Learning is mediated through tools and scaffolded interaction. AI can serve as a mediational tool, but the quality of scaffolding and gradual transfer of responsibility to the learner are the key educational questions.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://www.routledge.com/Theories-in-Second-Language-Acquisition/VanPatten-Keating-Wulff/p/book/9781138587380" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>58</td><td><strong>Boulton &amp; Cobb (2017)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Technology meta-analysis</span></td><td>Corpus use in language learning: A meta-analysis. Language Learning, 67(2), 348–393.</td><td>Corpus-based data-driven learning has a substantial positive effect, reminding AI designers that access to authentic, inspectable language evidence can be more pedagogically useful than opaque fluent answers.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1111/lang.12224" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>59</td><td><strong>Plonsky &amp; Oswald (2014)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">Methods</span></td><td>How big is ‘big’? Interpreting effect sizes in L2 research. Language Learning, 64(4), 878–912.</td><td>L2 effect sizes should be interpreted using field-sensitive benchmarks; very large early AI effects deserve caution when samples are small, heterogeneous or compared with weak controls.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1111/lang.12079" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>60</td><td><strong>Pack &amp; Maloney (2023)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">GenAI perspective</span></td><td>Using generative artificial intelligence for language education research: Insights from using OpenAI’s ChatGPT. TESOL Quarterly, 57(4), 1571–1582.</td><td>GenAI can assist language-education work, but reliability, transparency, prompt dependence and verification remain fundamental methodological concerns.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1002/tesq.3253" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>61</td><td><strong>Chapelle (2025)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">GenAI perspective</span></td><td>Generative AI as game changer: Implications for language education. System, 132, 103672.</td><td>GenAI changes access to interaction, language generation and feedback, but language-education value still depends on established SLA mechanisms and evidence rather than novelty.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.system.2025.103672" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>62</td><td><strong>Stockwell (2024)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">GenAI perspective</span></td><td>ChatGPT in language teaching and learning: Exploring the road we’re travelling. Technology in Language Teaching &amp; Learning, 6(1).</td><td>The paper urges language educators to treat ChatGPT as an evolving tool requiring AI literacy, explicit task design and critical evaluation rather than as an autonomous teacher.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.29140/tltl.v6n1.1176" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>63</td><td><strong>Alharbi (2023)</strong><span class="evidence-tag">AI writing review</span></td><td>AI in the foreign language classroom: A pedagogical overview of automated writing assistance tools. Education Research International, 2023, 4253331.</td><td>Automated writing assistants can provide scalable language feedback and revision support, while limitations in accuracy, depth and learner dependence justify human oversight.</td><td><a class="source-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1155/2023/4253331" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

<section class="takeaway" id="conclusion"><p class="section-kicker">Bottom line</p><h2>AI is most credible as a multiplier of practice, feedback and interaction</h2><p>Second-language learning still depends on the learner doing language-processing work. AI can make that work more available, individualized, immediate and repeatable. It can give a learner another pronunciation attempt, another conversation turn, another explanation, another revision cycle and another tailored example. Those are meaningful advantages. The evidence is much weaker for the idea that a fluent general-purpose model can replace curriculum, teachers, assessment standards, deliberate practice or real human communication. <strong>The design goal should therefore be AI that makes the learner do more useful language work—not AI that does the language work for the learner.</strong></p></section>

